    {
      "id": "e000030",
      "seq": 30,
      "runId": "build-72c2fffa-37de-4c82-b896-99fc0b35a4e2",
      "type": "design.note",
      "actor": "author",
      "origin": "agent_statement",
      "actionId": null,
      "candidateId": "v002",
      "details": {
        "goal": "Create one question in which the solver must determine separately the moving member's own rotation relative to the machine frame and the motion of its support around a fixed center. The solver must choose the correct reference frame first, and both observations must be determinable from the figure.",
        "sources": [
          "MentalAnimation/MechanicalSystem/Level0/5"
        ],
        "change": "A 20-tooth input drives the 30-tooth carrier gear in the rear plane. Its key drives H independently of the concentric frame-locked S. H carries the idler I and output P at two separated bearings. The separate input mesh requires the solver to infer H's orbital sense. Unequal end wheels prevent exact cancellation and make the observer choice decisive.",
        "reason": "Use clockwise as positive and normalize A to +1. A and B mesh on frame-located axles, so B and its keyed carrier H rotate at -20/30 = -2/3. The bearing of P therefore orbits counterclockwise. On H, the fixed S appears to spin at +2/3. The S-I and I-P meshes reverse this relative spin twice. Their tooth ratios telescope, giving P relative to H = (24/36)(2/3) = +4/9. Restore the fixed frame by adding H's -2/3. P spins at -2/9 while its bearing orbits at -2/3. Both observations are counterclockwise.",
        "expectedChecks": [
          "The five flags locate the input mesh, the B/H key, the frame lock on S, and both moving-axis meshes. The gear ratios, front views, input, outputs and four choices are legible.",
          "Blind read A / P CCW / H CCW."
        ]
      },
      "evidenceRefs": []
    },
